 "method": {
  "cold_ground": "Diviner's modelled annual maximum surface temperature below 110 K, the usual convention for a cold trap for water ice; 100 K and 120 K are given beside it.",
  "ice_stable": "Diviner's ice_depth: the depth at which water ice would sublimate at 1 kg per square metre per billion years. At the surface: code -1 (the surface stays below 101.3 K all year) or a depth of 0; buried: a depth no deeper than 0.5 m or 1 m; -999 means deeper than 2.8738 m or not stable at all and never counts.",
  "permanent_shadow": "Product 90's LPSR_80S_20MPP_ADJ: 20 m pixels the Sun never reaches over the Moon's 18.6-year cycle.",
  "slope": "Product 90's LDSM_80S_20MPP_ADJ, sampled every 10 m on the straight line from the site to the nearest ground of each kind; the steepest 20 m pixel crossed.",
  "grid": "Every layer on product 90's 20 m grid within 10 km of the site: a cell takes the value of the Diviner triangle its centre lies in. Areas count the cells whose centres lie within 1 or 5 km; distances are to the nearest cell centre of each kind, both corrected for the map's scale (under 0.8 % south of 80° S)."
 },
 "assumptions": "Diviner's model is a fit to the first mapping year on a Kaguya elevation model of triangles about 0.13 km² in area (pairs about 500 m across), with uniform thermal and reflectance properties; it does not resolve the small cold traps that hide in shadows a few metres to a few hundred metres across, and its mesh is registered to a different elevation model than product 90's. It gives where ice could last if it arrived, not whether ice is there, how much or how deep. The slope is along a straight line, not a route.",
